Chapter 13 – Part 2
The driver glanced at her in the rearview mirror, smiling with the easy confidence of someone who had lived long enough to recognize certain patterns.
“I said the same thing when I met my husband years ago—before we started dating.” A light, knowing pause. “Maybe… you plan to ask him out?”
Anri’s hands tightened slightly in her lap. The flush behind her glasses deepened as she looked down at them, then out the window again.
“…I don’t know,” she said softly. “It’s not like that. Or—I don’t think it is. He’s… kind. And he listens. And he doesn’t make me feel like I have to be anything other than what I am.”
A small, careful breath.
“But asking him out feels… big. I only just started understanding how to stand next to someone without disappearing into them. I don’t want to rush something just because it feels safe.”
She glanced toward the rearview mirror once, then away.
“For now I just want to keep talking to him. If it becomes more… I’ll know when it does.”
The driver gave a warm, quiet hum of understanding and didn’t push any further, leaving Anri to the soft darkness of the back seat and the unresolved thought still turning over in her mind—the Tech Rider’s words, Curtis’s voice, and the strange, quiet possibility that they might not be two different people at all.
The driver gave a knowing little smile in the rearview mirror.
“Just don’t wait too long. Great guys like that… may have someone gunning for him already.”
Anri’s face warmed instantly. The thought landed harder than she expected. The idea of someone else already reaching for the quiet space Curtis had made beside her made her chest tighten in a way she wasn’t ready to name.
And almost immediately, one person rose in her mind.
Rio.
She had seen them talking more than once—at school, in passing, the easy way Rio seemed to orbit him after whatever had happened between them. The memory sat uncomfortably in her stomach.
Anri looked down, needing something else to focus on, and slipped her hand into her pocket.
Her fingers brushed paper.
She pulled it out carefully. A small folded note. On it, a simple anime-style smiley face was drawn in pen, and beneath it a phone number written in clean, steady handwriting.
Curtis’s number.
A soft, involuntary smile touched her mouth before she could stop it. She unlocked her phone and saved it under his name, the glow of the screen reflecting faintly in her glasses.
From the front seat, the driver chuckled warmly.
“Ahh. I remember when we only had landlines. My husband did the same thing before we started dating. Slipped his number into my pocket when we hugged.” A pause, fond and amused. “Your friend did it when you hugged him before getting in the car.”
Anri’s blush deepened. She closed her fingers gently around the phone, the little paper still resting against it.
“…He did, didn’t he,” she murmured, more to herself than to the driver. Then, a little softer: “I didn’t even notice.”
She looked out the window again, the city lights sliding by, the newly saved number sitting quietly in her phone like a small, warm weight.
“…He’s always doing things like that,” she added, almost under her breath. “Helping. Giving. And then acting like it’s nothing.”
